# Cloudflare Pages redirects — PATHS ONLY.
#
# The host-to-host rule for crossword.thexigames.com used to live here and did
# nothing. Pages matches the source as a path, so a source written as a full
# URL never matched and the old subdomain quietly served the hub instead of the
# game. Every shared link would have landed on the wrong page.
#
# That redirect is a Cloudflare Redirect Rule on the zone instead:
#   Rules -> Redirect Rules -> hostname equals crossword.thexigames.com
#   Dynamic: concat("https://www.thexigames.com/crossword", http.request.uri.path)
#   301, Preserve query string ON
#
# Query string preservation is the part that matters: a shared result is
# /?token= and a challenge invite is /?c=.
#
# live_check.mjs follows the redirect and reads where it lands, so this cannot
# be broken without a suite saying so.

# Path-level redirects for this site go below.

# /account was linked from the first hub before any hub-level account page
# existed — the front door's Sign in led to a 404. The link is gone; this
# catches the address itself, which survives in histories and old tabs.
# 302 not 301: a hub account page is planned, and a cached permanent
# redirect would fight it when it arrives. Sign-in lives in the game.
/account   /football/crossword/   302

# ---- THE THEME MOVE, 5 Sep 2026 ----
#
# Every game moved under the theme it belongs to, because other kinds of quiz
# are coming and XI never meant football: eleven clues, eleven games, eleven
# players to a team. The old addresses must keep working forever — these games
# promise "one URL, one puzzle, forever", and a permalink that 404s breaks that
# promise in the one way it was built to prevent.
#
# 301 AND NOT 302 for the games: they have moved permanently, and search should
# consolidate on the new address. The splat carries the whole tail, so
# /crossword/daily/5 and /crossword/answers/2 and /hilo/clubs/ all land on
# their own new homes rather than on a game's front page — a redirect that
# drops the tail is a redirect that loses the board somebody was linking to.
/crossword/*    /football/crossword/:splat    301
/wordsearch/*   /football/wordsearch/:splat   301
/scrambled/*    /football/scrambled/:splat    301
/hilo/*         /football/hilo/:splat         301
/vowels/*       /football/vowels/:splat       301
/quickfire/*    /football/quickfire/:splat    301

# And the bare paths, which the splat above does not cover.
/crossword      /football/crossword/          301
/wordsearch     /football/wordsearch/         301
/scrambled      /football/scrambled/          301
/hilo           /football/hilo/               301
/vowels         /football/vowels/             301
/quickfire      /football/quickfire/          301

# THE THEME'S OWN PAGE, AND WHY THIS ONE IS A 302.
#
# The hub is at / and there is one theme, so /football/ has nothing of its own
# to say yet and points home. That will change the day a second theme lands: /
# becomes the picker and /football/ becomes the football hub, and this line is
# deleted.
#
# A redirect known to be temporary must not be permanent. It is on /football/
# rather than on / for the same reason: almost nobody has /football/ cached,
# and everybody has /. Putting the reversible redirect on the apex would have
# meant un-caching a 301 from the most-linked URL on the site.
/football/      /                             302

# WHAT PAGES SERVES THAT NOBODY MEANT IT TO — AND WHY THERE IS NO RULE HERE.
#
# Pages serves every path at the repo root that functions/ does not claim, so
# /tools/scrambled/xi/*.json was public: the Scrambled answer key and, worse,
# the SCHEDULE, on a game whose API answers 403 for any day past today because
# "the future is shut, because opening it gives away everything".
#
# The fix is that the bank now lives outside the repository, like the
# crossword's and the word search's. NOT a rule here: _redirects cannot 404 a
# path that exists as a static asset. Tried it —
#
#   /tools/*  /404.html  404
#
# and /tools/build_scrambled.js still answered 200 on a cache-busted request.
# Static assets are matched before redirects, so the rule was inert and the
# live_check assertion written against it could never have passed. Recorded
# here rather than deleted silently: the next person to reach for _redirects
# to hide a file should know it does not do that.
#
# The only thing that stops a file being served is not shipping it. If the
# remaining internals (tooling, migrations, suites) ever need hiding, the
# mechanism is _routes.json forcing those paths through the Worker, which is a
# routing change and wants its own release.
